{"id": "P02_cost_of_heat.w1", "model": "granite4.2:3b", "device": "cpu", "reasoning": "granite4.2:3b on the cpu", "prompt": "How was the 63.7 €/MWh cost of heat calculated?", "group": "provenance", "expected": "answer", "wall_seconds": 155.644, "warm_up_seconds": 4.575, "behaviour_ok": true, "checks": {"says one of gas": true, "provenance_used": true, "no_false_run_claim": true, "no_wrong_math": true, "no_refusal_with_data": true, "no_wrong_label": true, "no_stray_designs": true}, "pass": true, "result": {"behaviour": "answer", "asked": [], "answer": "The cost of heat is derived from the plant’s annual operating costs (wells, surface plant, treatment trains, heat pump, boiler, O&M, electricity, gas, CO₂) divided by the MWh of heat delivered. It reflects the sum of component costs per MWh as shown in the design block. **The 63.7 €/MWh**\nThe cost of heat, 63.7 €/MWh, is the sum of: Wells 7.6 €/MWh; Surface plant 8.4 €/MWh; Treatment trains 0.4 €/MWh; Heat pump 0.4 €/MWh; Boiler 1.5 €/MWh; Geothermal O&M 14.1 €/MWh; Heat pump O&M 0.1 €/MWh; Boiler O&M 1.1 €/MWh; Electricity: heat pump 0.8 €/MWh; Electricity: pumps 5.3 €/MWh; Gas 18.4 €/MWh; CO2: gas 4 €/MWh; CO2: produced gas 1.8 €/MWh. The search tried heat pumps from 0.2 to 1.5 MW: 0.5 MW was among the cheapest (58.2 €/MWh of heat and cold, against 58.5 €/MWh at 1.5 MW: past that, the extra capital, O&M and electricity cost more than the gas and CO2 a larger heat pump saves).", "ungrounded": [], "dropped": ["depth 3000 m (not written as a depth)", "reservoir_depth_m=3000 (not written as one)"], "verified": null, "reasoning": true, "reasoning_auto": true, "device": "cpu", "decided_by": {"think": "model", "answer": "model"}, "message": "How was the 63.7 €/MWh cost of heat calculated?", "intent": "explain", "language": "en", "screened": false, "parameters": {"changes": {}, "doublets": 0, "unsupported": [], "dropped": [], "failures": [], "designs": [], "steps": []}, "provenance": ["lcoh", "heat_split"], "claims_checked"