{"id": "P02_cost_of_heat.w3", "model": "granite4.2:3b", "device": "cpu", "reasoning": "granite4.2:3b on the cpu", "prompt": "What makes up the 63.7?", "group": "provenance", "expected": "answer", "wall_seconds": 149.176, "warm_up_seconds": 4.575, "behaviour_ok": true, "checks": {"says one of gas": true, "provenance_used": true, "no_false_run_claim": true, "no_wrong_math": true, "no_refusal_with_data": true, "no_wrong_label": true, "no_stray_designs": true}, "pass": true, "result": {"behaviour": "answer", "asked": [], "answer": "**The 63.7 €/MWh**\nThe cost of heat, 63.7 €/MWh, is the sum of: Wells 7.6 €/MWh; Surface plant 8.4 €/MWh; Treatment trains 0.4 €/MWh; Heat pump 0.4 €/MWh; Boiler 1.5 €/MWh; Geothermal O&M 14.1 €/MWh; Heat pump O&M 0.1 €/MWh; Boiler O&M 1.1 €/MWh; Electricity: heat pump 0.8 €/MWh; Electricity: pumps 5.3 €/MWh; Gas 18.4 €/MWh; CO2: gas 4 €/MWh; CO2: produced gas 1.8 €/MWh. Each part is a year's cost (the capital's annuity at 5.2 % real over each unit's life, fixed and variable O&M, gas and electricity at sourced prices, CO2 at the carbon price) divided by the MWh delivered; shared costs are split by the brine heat each side uses.\n\n**The 63.7%**\nWhy the heat splits as it does: the exchanger gives at most 12.23 MW (1 doublet at up to 300 m3/h each), and it can heat the 60 °C return only to 95.7 °C (the brine at the wellhead less the exchanger's approach), 89.3% of the way to 100 °C; the heat demand peaks at 30 MW. The heat pump (0.5 MW) takes the brine after the exchanger as its source. The search tried heat pumps from 0.2 to 1.5 MW: 0.5 MW was among the cheapest (58.2 €/MWh of heat and cold, against 58.5 €/MWh at 1.5 MW: past that, the extra capital, O&M and electricity cost more than the gas and CO2 a larger heat pump saves). The boiler covers the rest: 38.5 GWh, 34.5% of the year's heat. A reliable plan (N-1) adds standby units only; it does not change where the heat comes from.", "ungrounded": [], "dropped": [], "verified": null, "reasoni